---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Privacy Policy" description="Privacy policy for Chief of Staff (agent-platform), Tim Samson's personal assistant app.">
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-16">

    <header class="mb-12">
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-black leading-tight mb-6">Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">Chief of Staff (agent-platform) &middot; Updated November 1, 2026</p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose prose-lg prose-gray max-w-none prose-headings:font-black">
      <p>
        Chief of Staff is a personal assistant that Tim Samson built and runs for his own use. It is not
        offered to the public, and it is not a commercial product.
      </p>

      <h2>What it reads</h2>
      <p>
        When you sign in with Google or Microsoft, the app asks for read-only access to that account's
        mail and calendar (for Google: <code>gmail.readonly</code> and <code>calendar.readonly</code>).
        It cannot send, delete, or change anything in your account.
      </p>

      <h2>What it does with it</h2>
      <p>
        It reads new mail and calendar entries to summarize them and flag what needs attention for the
        account's owner. Message content is processed by an AI model provider to produce those summaries.
        Nothing is sold, used for advertising, or shared with anyone else.
      </p>

      <h2>Where it's kept</h2>
      <p>
        Sign-in tokens and the assistant's working data are stored encrypted on a private server
        controlled by Tim Samson. Logs and alerts hold codes and IDs only, not message content. Data is
        deleted on the app's retention schedule, and an account's sign-in token is destroyed when the
        account is disconnected.
      </p>

      <h2>Google user data</h2>
      <p>
        The app's use of information received from Google APIs adheres to the
        <a href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy">Google API Services User Data Policy</a>,
        including the Limited Use requirements.
      </p>

      <h2>Removing access</h2>
      <p>
        You can revoke access at any time from your
        <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/permissions">Google account permissions</a> or your
        Microsoft account's app permissions page. Questions: use the support address shown on the
        app's sign-in screen.
      </p>
    </div>

  </article>
</Layout>
